<!-- ===================== HOW I WORK ===================== -->
<section class="section section--tight wrap">
  <p class="eyebrow" style="color:var(--blue)">How I work</p>
  <h2 class="headline">Good product work is unglamorous and repeatable.</h2>

  <div style="margin-top:2rem">
    <div class="principle">
      <h3>Systems thinking came before design.</h3>
      <p class="prose">I didn&rsquo;t start in a design studio. I started taking things apart, and not long after, building websites for anyone who would let me. Somewhere past a hundred of them you stop seeing pages and start seeing systems &mdash; the same handful of problems wearing different visual clothes. Before I ask what a screen should look like, I want to know what the system underneath it is actually doing, because most product problems that present as design problems aren&rsquo;t.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="principle">
      <h3>Vision and delivery are the same job.</h3>
      <p class="prose">Product development is a cycle: identify the problem, explore solutions, test, gather feedback, change what&rsquo;s wrong, ship to real people, measure what happened. A strategy that never reaches that last step is a document, not a strategy. I stay close enough to delivery to know whether the plan survived contact with reality.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="principle">
      <h3>AI belongs in the product, not the pitch deck.</h3>
      <p class="prose">The interesting question isn&rsquo;t whether you can add AI &mdash; it&rsquo;s whether it changes the outcome. Predictive ordering that actually reduces waste. An internal QA tool that cuts critical errors. Story generation and code review that give a team back real hours. If it doesn&rsquo;t move a number someone cares about, it&rsquo;s decoration.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="principle">
      <h3>Hard data and soft data both count.</h3>
      <p class="prose">I integrate quantitative and qualitative signal into every plan I write. Analytics tell you what happened; conversations tell you why. Teams that only trust the first ship confidently in the wrong direction, and teams that only trust the second never find out whether they were right.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===================== IN MY OWN WORDS ===================== -->
<section class="band">
  <div class="wrap">
    <p class="eyebrow">In my own words</p>
    <h2 class="headline">It started with a soldering iron, not a studio.</h2>

    <div class="measure prose" style="margin-top:2rem">
      <p>My path into product didn&rsquo;t run through design school. It ran through MySpace. I was customizing profile pages with HTML and CSS &mdash; learning by breaking things and then working out how to fix them &mdash; and that was the first time it landed that you could type something and change what another person saw. I never really stopped.</p>

      <p>Twenty years and more than 150 websites later, freelance work has run alongside every job I&rsquo;ve held. It&rsquo;s the part of my career that never had a title. Building for clients teaches you something no spec ever will: nobody actually wants a website. They want the thing the website does for them. Learning to hear the second thing when someone asks for the first is most of what product management turns out to be.</p>

      <p>Marketplaces, then enterprise consulting, then payments, then fintech, then AI-native product work &mdash; on paper it reads like drift. The through line is that I&rsquo;ve been shipping to real users the entire time, and each sector taught me a different way things break. Dominion taught me scale. Cognizant taught me how large organisations actually make decisions. PayPal taught me what nine markets and a compliance review do to a roadmap. Relief Financial taught me how little you know at the start.</p>

      <p>What&rsquo;s stayed constant is the part I enjoy most: the space between a rough idea and a polished product. It isn&rsquo;t about making things look good. It&rsquo;s about building systems that work, solving real problems for real people, and making software that feels less like software and more like a well-made tool.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ===================== FAQ ===================== -->
